    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> religion <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a> descended on a chain, cast earth on the primordial ocean and founded Ilé-Ifẹ̀ as the first city; his sons, daughters and grandchildren went out to found Ila Orangun, Owu, Ketu, Sabe, Egba, Popo, Ijesha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oranmiyan">Oranmiyan</a> the second dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>.</li><li>Between 1200 and 1400 the artists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> made naturalistic sculptures in terracotta, stone and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> alloy, many under the patronage of King Obalufon II, honoured as the inventor of bronze casting in a life-size copper mask.</li><li>The sacred grove of Igbo Olokun held one of the earliest known glass workshops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, at its peak from the eleventh to the fifteenth century; its high-lime, high-alumina <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> have been found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a>, Essouk and Kissi.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> is called the city of 401 deities: the Ooni is counted the 401st <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a>, the only one that speaks, and traditional worshippers are said to celebrate a festival of one deity on every day of the year.</p></aside>
    </section>

