    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ilé-Ifẹ̀ is an ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, founded at some time between 1000 and 500 BCE; by 900 CE it had become an important West African emporium producing sophisticated art. Its own account of its origin is the Yoruba story of the creation of the world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olorun">Olodumare</a>, the Supreme God, ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obatala">Obatala</a> to create the earth, but Obatala drank too much palm wine on the way, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a> took the items of creation from him, descended from the abode of the orisha on a chain and cast a handful of earth on the primordial ocean. The earth rose as a mound, Oke Ora; a five-toed cockerel scattered it to make the land on which the first city would be built, and a palm nut Oduduwa planted sprang up as a tree with sixteen branches, the sixteen clans of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> pre-urban confederation, the Elu Merindinlogun.</p><p>The name Ife means 'expansion', and Ile-Ife 'the land', or in modern Yoruba 'the home', 'of expansion'. Oduduwa's usurpation of creation began an everlasting rivalry with Obatala, which the votaries of the two divinities still re-enact at the Itapa New Year festival; Oduduwa became the ancestor of the first divine king of the Yoruba, and Obatala is believed to have made the first Yoruba people from clay. Oduduwa created a dynasty at Ife, and the first Ooni was his descendant. The Ooni is traditionally counted the 401st orisha, the only one that speaks, and the royal dynasty traces its origin to the founding of the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Agriculture occupied most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a>'s people, and the region still produces vegetables, grain, cocoa, tobacco and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>. Between the twelfth and fourteenth centuries the city was a settlement of substantial size, its houses laid with potsherd pavements. Iron was smelted in the Ife region: excavations at Iyekere have found burnt tuyères, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a>, decorated potsherds, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a> and slag, and the smelting was efficient, working ore of nearly 80 per cent iron oxide and leaving a lean slag.</p><p>The city's most far-reaching industry was glass. In the sacred grove of Igbo Olokun, where the goddess Olokun was worshipped, glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and production materials have been excavated, and analysis dates the main working to the eleventh to fifteenth centuries, perhaps beginning earlier; it may be one of the earliest glass workshops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, and its recipes, raw materials and pyrotechnology were largely independent of traditions farther afield. Two compositions, high-lime high-alumina and low-lime high-alumina glass, were developed from local materials. Beads of the first kind found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a> and Essouk in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and at Kissi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a> after the ninth century show the industry's part in regional trade, including the trans-Saharan trade. In the Osun Grove the technology persisted into the seventeenth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sources for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> say little of war; the city's power was spiritual and dynastic, and its reach was carried by its princes rather than its armies. In tradition the Ooni was head of the spiritual, political and military affairs of all the lands before the other kingdoms went out from Ife to establish their own territories, and every other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> ruler is called igbakeji <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a>, second to the orisha who is the Ooni. The dispersal from the holy city was the kingdom's expansion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a>'s sons, daughters and grandchildren founded their own kingdoms and empires: Ila Orangun, Owu, Ketu, Sabe, Egba, Popo, Ijesha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oranmiyan">Oranmiyan</a>, Oduduwa's last-born and one of his father's principal ministers, was sent to govern the nascent Edo kingdom when its people asked for Oduduwa's rule. When he returned to Ile-Ife after a period of service in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> he left behind Eweka, his son by Erinmwinde, daughter of the Ogie of Egor, who became the first widely accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> of the second Edo dynasty that has ruled Benin ever since. Oranmiyan then went north-west into the savanna to found Oyo, which grew into an empire stretching at its height from the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> to the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-river">Volta</a>, one of the most powerful of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>'s medieval states until its collapse in the mid-nineteenth century. Those two creations of Ife's dynasty, Benin and Oyo, became the military powers of the region, and after 1400 political and economic power passed from Ife to them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> was ruled by the Ooni, the title of the god-king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a>, which every king of Ife took after his reign. The Ooni was the spiritual and political leader of the people later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> and the head of spiritual, political and military affairs across all the lands before the other kingdoms were founded; he is counted the 401st <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a>, and all other Yoruba kings are 'igbakeji orisha', second in command to him. He is the chief priest and custodian of the holy city of all the Yoruba. Historically the king appeared in public only once a year, at the Olojo festival, the celebration of the new dawn.</p><p>Religion was the frame of rule. Ife is called the city of 401 deities, irunmole or orisha, and a festival of one of them is said to fall on every day of the year, often extending over several days and joining priestly rites in the palace to theatrical dramatisations in the rest of the kingdom: the Itapa festival for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obatala">Obatala</a> and Obameri, the Edi festival for Moremi Ajasoro, the Ugbo with their Igare masqueraders. Shrines and groves held the cults: the Oduduwa Shrine and Grove of the progenitor of the Yoruba, the Agbonniregun Temple of Orunmila, orisha of wisdom and divination, and Igbo Olokun. In art, kings and gods were shown with large heads because the ase, a person's inner power, was believed to be held there, and both historic figures and the offices they held were represented.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> is the holy city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> and the source of their royal lines. By its traditions the dynasties of Ila Orangun, Owu, Ketu, Sabe, Egba, Popo, Ijesha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a> descend from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a>, and through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oranmiyan">Oranmiyan</a>'s son Eweka the second dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> as well; Oyo became one of the most powerful of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>'s medieval states, and Benin a major empire. The Ooni is regarded as chief priest and custodian of the holy city of all the Yoruba, and since the Yoruba <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">Orisha</a> Congress of 1986 he has held an international standing the title had not had since the city's colonisation by the British.</p><p>Ife's art is what the world chiefly knows of it. Its naturalistic bronze, stone and terracotta sculptures, which reached their peak between 1200 and 1400, are significant examples of naturalism in pre-colonial African art, distinguished by their regalia, facial marking patterns and body proportions; the life-size <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mask of King Obalufon II, honoured as the Yoruba patron deity of brass casting, weaving and regalia, is the best documented. Its glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> travelled farther still, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a> and Essouk in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, to Kissi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, as a currency of power and belief for Yoruba, West Africans and, in time, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-diaspora">African diaspora</a>. The city is now home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obafemi-awolowo">Obafemi Awolowo</a> University and Oduduwa University, and remains, as the city of 401 deities, the place where the orisha are celebrated through the year.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a>'s eminence as a centre of power did not outlast its art. After the sculptural tradition peaked between 1200 and 1400, production declined as political and economic power shifted to the nearby kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, which, like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a>, developed into a major empire. Both were in tradition the creations of Ife's own dynasty, and both outgrew it: Oyo at its height stretched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-river">Volta</a> before it collapsed in the mid-nineteenth century. The glass industry of Igbo Olokun passed its peak in the fifteenth century, though the distinct Yoruba technology persisted in the Osun Grove into the seventeenth. Ife kept what the empires could not take, the sacred standing of the Ooni and the cults of its 401 deities.</p><p>The city was colonised by the British, after which the holders of the Ooni's title lost the wider standing they would regain only in 1986 with the Yoruba <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">Orisha</a> Congress. The throne itself continued: Adesoji Aderemi, Atobatele I (1889–1980), was the 49th Ooni; Okunade Sijuwade, Olubuse II (1930–2015), the 50th; and since 2015 Adeyeye Enitan Ogunwusi, Ojaja II, a Nigerian accountant born in 1974, has reigned as the 51st. Today Ife is a city of more than 500,000 people, the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osun-state">Osun State</a> by the 2006 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, divided between the Ife East and Ife Central local government areas; the Ooni is prominent among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>'s royal obas, and the festivals of the orisha continue.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Ilé-Ifẹ̀ is the holy city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a> founded the dynasty from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> and the other Yoruba kingdoms claim descent. Ruled by the Ooni, it produced between 1200 and 1400 the naturalistic bronze, stone and terracotta sculptures and the glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> for which it is famous, before power passed to Benin and Oyo.</em></p>

